1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:04,750
BookLeaf's authors now get help right inside their own dashboard.

2
00:00:05,140 --> 00:00:09,150
Any product can do the same, with one script tag, and one endpoint.

3
00:00:10,400 --> 00:00:13,990
Here's how to plug BookLeaf Support into any product.

4
00:00:15,300 --> 00:00:17,570
Your page loads one script.

5
00:00:18,000 --> 00:00:20,830
Your server says who the customer is, with a short-lived token.

6
00:00:20,580 --> 00:00:24,330
And support reads facts from the APIs you already have.

7
00:00:24,080 --> 00:00:25,790
Nothing is copied.

8
00:00:27,300 --> 00:00:29,790
Step one: create your workspace.

9
00:00:30,140 --> 00:00:32,250
Its id becomes your widget's address.

10
00:00:33,300 --> 00:00:37,650
You get a checklist that ticks itself, as each step really starts working.

11
00:00:38,800 --> 00:00:40,950
Step two: connect your data.

12
00:00:41,540 --> 00:00:47,970
Add your API key and secret, list your data sources, and press Test to see exactly what comes back.

13
00:00:48,800 --> 00:00:52,930
Step three: map your fields to the JSON your API already sends.

14
00:00:53,360 --> 00:00:55,730
These are the only figures the AI is allowed to quote.

15
00:00:56,800 --> 00:01:04,510
Step four is the only real code: one endpoint, behind your own login, that signs a five-minute token for the current customer.

16
00:01:05,800 --> 00:01:12,810
That token works once, expires fast, stays out of server logs, and can only sign in your own customers.

17
00:01:14,300 --> 00:01:19,170
Step five: paste one script tag, and list the websites allowed to show it.

18
00:01:20,800 --> 00:01:21,910
And that's it.

19
00:01:22,060 --> 00:01:25,930
The widget just appears, already signed in, in your brand colours.

20
00:01:26,800 --> 00:01:29,670
Step six: teach the AI.

21
00:01:30,340 --> 00:01:34,590
Add your categories, your knowledge articles, and how replies should sound.

22
00:01:35,800 --> 00:01:36,650
Want more?

23
00:01:36,940 --> 00:01:42,690
Scoped API keys let your backend create and read tickets, with every route documented in Swagger.

24
00:01:43,800 --> 00:01:50,010
And signed webhooks tell your server about every new ticket, reply, and status change.

25
00:01:51,300 --> 00:01:55,430
Send a test ticket, watch it land in your queue, and you're live.

26
00:01:56,900 --> 00:01:58,430
One script tag.

27
00:01:58,640 --> 00:01:59,450
One endpoint.

28
00:01:59,760 --> 00:02:01,210
Everything else is configuration.
